Examples
# In a package's R/zzz.R, as use_jlbind() writes it
.onLoad &lt;- function(libname, pkgname) {
  jlbind::bind_client(pkgname)
}

Declaring entrypoints

@rcall
@rcall f(arg::T, ...)::R
Make f callable from R. jlbind::update_entrypoints() writes a matching R function for each @rcall.
greet(name) = "Hello, " * name * "!"

@rcall greet(name::String)::String
Every argument and the return value need a type, which decides how they are converted (see from_sexp and to_sexp). Use RRef{T} to give R a Julia object by reference. An argument of type Union{Nothing,T} accepts NULL, and Union{Missing,T} accepts NA.
An argument can have a default, which the R function gets too: a number, string, true, false, missing (NA), nothing (NULL), or a vector of these such as [1, 2].
If f throws, R gets an error. The message of an ArgumentError, error() and a few other common exceptions is passed on; others get a generic message.
Write each @rcall at the top level of a file, as update_entrypoints() reads the source.

Converting values
@rcall converts arguments and return values with these.

from_sexp
from_sexp(T, x::SEXP) -&gt; T
Convert the R object x to a T, or throw an ArgumentError saying what was expected. @rcall converts every argument with it; add a method to accept a type of your own.
It supports:

String and Symbol (from a string or factor), Bool, Float64, Int64, Int32, ComplexF64, Date, DateTime and the smaller number types, and vectors of all of these;
Union{Missing,T} and Vector{Union{Missing,T}}, which allow NA;
Union{Nothing,T}, which allows NULL;
Matrix{T} and Array{T,N}, from an R matrix or array;
other Vectors, Tuples, NamedTuples and Dict{String,T}, from a list;
RVector{T}, RDataFrame, and SEXP, which is passed as is.



to_sexp
to_sexp(x) -&gt; SEXP
Convert x to an R object. @rcall converts every return value with it; add a method to return a type of your own.
It supports the types from_sexp does, plus nothing (NULL) and missing (NA). An Int64 becomes a double, since R’s integers have 32 bits: return an Int32 for an R integer.


RVector
RVector{T}
A read-only view of the data of an R vector, without a copy: RVector{Float64} for a double vector, RVector{Int32} for an integer vector and RVector{UInt8} for a raw vector. Use it for a large vector you only read.
NA is not checked for, and the view is only valid during the call, so don’t keep it. Returning it returns the R vector it views.


RDataFrame
RDataFrame(columns::NamedTuple)
A data frame, converted to and from an R data.frame (or tibble). Its columns are df.x and so on. As an argument, the data frame must have exactly these columns, in any order.
const Sales = RDataFrame{(:group, :amount),Tuple{Vector{String},Vector{Float64}}}

total(df) = sum(df.amount)

@rcall total(df::Sales)::Float64

Trimming

@narrow
@narrow x (A, B, ...) expr [otherwise]
Evaluate expr with x known to be an A, a B, and so on, whichever it is. If it is none of them, evaluate otherwise, or throw an ArgumentError.
The compiled library needs this to call methods on an argument of an abstract type:
shape_area(@nospecialize(s::Shape)) = @narrow s (Circle, Square) area(s)
